# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-

def blocks():
    return [
        {"t": "h1", "text": "Day 2 — SOLID and design patterns"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Outcome: you can answer the classic pattern question a traditional panel expects, and you can show a product panel that you choose the smallest design that keeps the next change local."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Time box (3.5 hours)"},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "30 min: SOLID, one Web API example each, spoken.",
            "40 min: pattern catalog table. Cover the name, the problem, and the refusal.",
            "90 min: Q1 to Q8 aloud.",
            "40 min: take one service you know and mark which patterns are actually present. Most codebases need three, not fifteen.",
        ]},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "SOLID on a Web API"},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "Single responsibility: a controller action translates HTTP. A handler performs the use case. EF mapping stays at the data edge. A class that validates, calculates tax, and sends email has three reasons to change.",
            "Open/closed: a new shipping provider is a new class behind IShippingRate, registered in DI. The checkout handler stays closed. I do not invent a plugin system for two branches of an if.",
            "Liskov: a subtype honors the contract. An override that throws NotSupportedException for a base method will surprise every caller. Prefer a smaller interface.",
            "Interface segregation: IOrderQueries and IOrderCommands, or a single focused IClock, beat one IOrderService with forty methods. Callers depend on the slice they use.",
            "Dependency inversion: the handler depends on an interface it owns. The SQL or HTTP adapter implements it. High-level policy does not reference a concrete SDK type.",
        ]},
        {"t": "lead", "title": "How to say SOLID in an interview", "text": "Give one example from an API you shipped. Then say which letter you violate on purpose in a small service, because a one-class endpoint is easier to operate than a six-project solution. Judgment is the signal."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Pattern catalog for this role"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Learn the spoken answer, not a UML sheet. If they only want the classic name, give the first sentence. If they are senior, give the refusal column."},
        {"t": "table", "headers": ["Pattern", "Say this", "A .NET shape", "Refuse it when"], "rows": [
            ["Singleton", "One instance, controlled creation.", "DI singleton lifetime.", "The type holds request data, or you hand-roll a lock around a static instance."],
            ["Factory", "Creation is a decision worth naming.", "A typed HttpClient factory, or a small factory method.", "The factory only calls new and has one implementation."],
            ["Abstract factory", "A family of related objects is created together.", "Rare in CRUD APIs. Sometimes a per-tenant infrastructure set.", "You have one product, not a family."],
            ["Builder", "Step-by-step construction of a complex object.", "WebApplicationBuilder, or a test-data builder.", "The object has two constructor parameters."],
            ["Adapter", "Make an outside API look like your interface.", "A class that wraps a payment SDK.", "You wrap a type you own and control."],
            ["Decorator", "Add behavior around an existing interface.", "Caching or timing around IPriceService; middleware is the HTTP form.", "The extra behavior is a single line the caller should see."],
            ["Facade", "One entry hides a messy subsystem.", "An application service in front of three legacy calls.", "The facade becomes a new god class."],
            ["Proxy", "A stand-in controls access to the real object.", "EF lazy-loading proxy; a caching proxy.", "You need the real object and the proxy hides a query."],
            ["Strategy", "Swap an algorithm behind one interface.", "ITaxCalculator per region, chosen by DI or a key.", "Two strategies and the choice is a single if."],
            ["Observer", "Dependents hear a change.", "C# events in-process; a message on Service Bus across processes.", "You need a guaranteed cross-service workflow. Use a message with a consumer contract."],
            ["Command", "Represent a request as an object.", "A MediatR IRequest, or a queue message.", "The command has no value beyond a method call."],
            ["Chain of responsibility", "Pass a request along handlers until one handles it.", "ASP.NET Core middleware and the filter pipeline.", "A chain of three classes that always all run. That is a pipeline you should write plainly."],
            ["Template method", "A base algorithm with replaceable steps.", "A base importer with abstract Parse and Save.", "The base class forces unused steps. Prefer composition."],
            ["Mediator", "Objects talk through a mediator.", "MediatR, or a single dispatcher you wrote.", "Every call is a mediator message, including trivial reads, and stack traces become noise."],
            ["Repository", "A collection-like boundary over persistence.", "A focused data port per aggregate.", "The repository only forwards DbSet methods one-to-one."],
            ["Unit of Work", "One business operation commits as one unit.", "DbContext is already a unit of work.", "A second unit-of-work wrapper around one DbContext."],
            ["Specification", "Encapsulate a business predicate.", "A testable expression reused by a query.", "A specification engine for one Where clause."],
            ["Options", "Bind configuration to a typed object.", "IOptions<T>, IOptionsSnapshot<T>, IOptionsMonitor<T>.", "Services read IConfiguration strings from deep inside the domain."],
            ["Result", "Return success or failure without an exception for expected cases.", "A Result<T> for validation and domain rule failures.", "You hide defects. A bug still throws."],
            ["CQRS", "Separate the write model from the read model.", "Different handlers, sometimes different stores.", "Two models for a form with three fields."],
            ["Outbox", "Save state and the message in one database transaction.", "A row in Outbox, a relay to Service Bus.", "You publish to the bus and then save, hoping both work."],
            ["Saga", "A long business process with compensation.", "Durable Functions orchestration, or a process manager.", "A single local transaction would do."],
            ["Circuit breaker", "Stop calling a dependency that is failing.", "Resilience pipeline on HttpClient.", "You break the circuit on your own database for a single timeout without a policy."],
            ["Strangler fig", "Replace a legacy system one route at a time.", "A facade that routes some URLs to the new API.", "A big-bang rewrite with no production traffic on the new path."],
            ["Anti-corruption layer", "Translate a legacy model so it does not leak.", "A mapper at the boundary of the old database or WCF service.", "You share the legacy entity project to go faster."],
        ]},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "If they name a pattern you rarely use"},
        {"t": "p", "text": "Have a one-line honest answer. Bluffing a Visitor diagram is worse than a calm scope statement."},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "Prototype: clone an object to avoid expensive setup. I copy DTOs explicitly. I rarely need a prototype registry.",
            "Flyweight: share immutable intrinsic state. String interning is the example I cite. I do not design it into CRUD.",
            "Bridge: split abstraction from implementation so both vary. I usually just inject an interface.",
            "Composite: treat a tree of parts like one part. A category tree or a menu. Uncommon in a flat Web API.",
            "State: behavior changes with state. An order state machine belongs in a named type or a constrained status transition, not a pile of flags.",
            "Memento: capture and restore state. I use persisted workflow state or an audit table.",
            "Visitor: add operations over a fixed type hierarchy. I mention it for compilers and document trees, then say I have shipped it rarely.",
            "Interpreter: a grammar and an evaluator. I have used expression trees via EF, and I do not write interpreters for business rules unless the business truly authors rules.",
        ]},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Implement a thread-safe Singleton, then tell me why your API does not do that.",
         "a": "The classic form is a private constructor, a private static readonly instance created by the runtime, and a public static accessor. Static initialization in .NET is thread-safe for that pattern. The lazy form uses Lazy<T> with LazyThreadSafetyMode.ExecutionAndPublication. Double-checked locking is the interview variant I can explain and hope never to maintain. In ASP.NET Core I register the service with AddSingleton. The container creates one instance for the process, and the type must be thread-safe. I still do not put per-user or per-request data there.",
         "lead": "A handwritten singleton hides the dependency. Tests cannot replace it. A second App Service instance is a second singleton, so it is not a single instance of the business. Cross-instance uniqueness belongs in the database with a unique constraint."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Factory versus Strategy. Give an example from checkout.",
         "a": "A factory decides how to build an object. A strategy decides how to do a behavior. At checkout I might have a ShippingLabelFactory that knows which carrier client to construct from configuration. I might have an ITaxCalculator strategy with one implementation per region. The handler depends on ITaxCalculator. Selection can be a keyed service in DI, or a small resolver that maps a country code to an implementation. I keep the resolver dumb. The tax rule lives in the strategy, where it can be tested without HTTP.",
         "lead": "If the team has two calculators and the choice is one if, I leave the if in the handler. I extract a strategy when the third country arrives, or when the rule needs its own tests. Premature strategies scatter a simple policy."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Where do Decorator, middleware, and filters each belong?",
         "a": "Middleware sits on the HTTP pipeline and runs for requests that reach it. I use it for correlation ids, exception handling to ProblemDetails, security headers, and request logging. It should be short. Filters sit closer to MVC actions: authorization filters, validation filters, and result filters. A decorator wraps an application interface, such as IPriceService, and adds caching or metrics without the price service knowing. I pick middleware when the concern is HTTP. I pick a filter when it is controller-specific. I pick a decorator when the concern is a use case that might also run from a queue consumer with no HTTP at all.",
         "lead": "Business rules in middleware become invisible. I have seen discount logic in a middleware because it needed the user header. That logic moves into the handler, with a test that does not spin up the host."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Do you use a generic repository and a unit of work with EF Core?",
         "a": "DbContext is a unit of work. DbSet is a repository. A second generic repository that exposes Get, Add, and a predicate usually hides none of the complexity and blocks the features I need: Include shape, split queries, projections, and ExecuteUpdate. I use a focused port when the domain must stay ignorant of EF, for example IOrderStore with GetById and Save of an aggregate. The implementation is a thin EF class in the infrastructure project. One SaveChanges per use case commits the unit. I add an explicit transaction when one use case writes through two contexts or needs a SQL lock hint I can explain.",
         "lead": "I will defend a thin repository at an aggregate boundary in a modular solution. I will not defend IRepository<T> as a standard. It is ceremony that makes bad queries harder to see in review."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Explain CQRS and Mediator as you would use them on a real API.",
         "a": "CQRS means the write path and the read path can differ. On a small API that is just two handlers and two queries, still on the same database. On a hot read model it can be a projection updated from events, with the understanding that the read side lags. I do not split databases until the read and write need different scaling or different shapes. Mediator is a way to dispatch a command or query object to one handler. MediatR is the common library. It gives a pipeline for logging, validation, and transactions. The cost is indirection: a new developer presses F12 and lands on an interface. I use it when the pipeline behaviors earn their keep. I skip it when the controller can call one application service that is already clear.",
         "lead": "I introduce MediatR with a written rule: commands change state, queries do not, handlers do not call other handlers, and cross-cutting work is a pipeline behavior. Without that rule the mediator becomes a web of hidden calls."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "Options pattern: which interface do you inject?",
         "a": "I bind a configuration section to a class and validate it on start with ValidateOnStart, so a bad setting fails deployment and not the first request. IOptions<T> is a singleton snapshot read once. IOptionsSnapshot<T> is scoped and sees changes on the next request, which fits a normal Web API that reloads configuration. IOptionsMonitor<T> is for singletons that must observe changes, such as a hosted service. I inject the options type the lifetime requires. I do not pass IConfiguration into the domain. Secrets come from Key Vault through configuration, and the options class exposes only what the feature needs.",
         "lead": "A missing ValidateOnStart is how a slot swap succeeds and the app crashes on the first order. Startup validation is a release check, not a style preference."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "When do you return a Result, and when do you throw?",
         "a": "I throw for defects and for situations the caller cannot handle: a broken invariant, a null that the type system promised would exist, an IO failure after retries. I return a Result, or a validation problem, for expected business outcomes: invalid input, insufficient stock, a duplicate idempotency key. Validation failures become HTTP 400 with ProblemDetails. A conflict becomes 409. An exception I did not expect becomes 500 and a logged exception with a correlation id. I do not catch Exception at the bottom of every method. One exception handler middleware does that translation.",
         "lead": "I watch for Result types that include a stack trace and an error code for bugs. That hides failures from the hosting model and from crash telemetry. Expected no, unexpected throw."},
        {"t": "qa", "q": "How do you decide to add a pattern during a design review?",
         "a": "I ask what change we are protecting, who feels the pain, and what the pattern costs in navigation and tests. If the change is speculative, I keep the code in one type. If the change has already happened twice, I extract an interface at the seam that varied. I also ask how the failure will look in logs. A pattern that adds three layers and no correlation id has made the system harder to operate. I write the decision in the PR or a short ADR: context, decision, consequences. A tech lead's pattern choice is a team rule, so the next person can repeat it.",
         "lead": "I keep an ADR folder with one page per real decision. In the interview I offer to walk one: why we kept a modular monolith, why we chose Service Bus, or why we dropped a generic repository. That story is more convincing than a pattern list."},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "A solution shape you can draw"},
        {"t": "b", "items": [
            "Api project: controllers or endpoints, auth, middleware, composition root.",
            "Application project: use cases, ports, validators. No EF reference if you are serious about the boundary.",
            "Domain project: entities, value objects, domain rules. No ASP.NET reference.",
            "Infrastructure project: EF Core, HTTP adapters, Service Bus. Implements ports.",
            "Tests: domain tests with no host; a smaller set of API tests with WebApplicationFactory.",
        ]},
        {"t": "p", "text": "For a small service I collapse Application and Domain, and I say so. Four projects for eight classes is how a lead loses the team."},
        {"t": "code", "text": "builder.Services.AddScoped<IOrderStore, EfOrderStore>();\nbuilder.Services.AddScoped<CreateOrderHandler>();\nbuilder.Services.AddOptions<PricingOptions>()\n    .Bind(builder.Configuration.GetSection(\"Pricing\"))\n    .ValidateDataAnnotations()\n    .ValidateOnStart();"},
        {"t": "h2", "text": "Evening drill"},
        {"t": "b", "numbered": True, "items": [
            "Pick three patterns you have shipped. Tell the problem, the shape, and the day you would remove each.",
            "Draw the four-project solution. Then cross out a project and justify a simpler layout for a two-person service.",
            "Explain generic repository in the classic way, then give the EF Core answer. Some panels want both. Lead with the one that matches the interviewer, and offer the other.",
        ]},
        {"t": "break"},
    ]
